-- title:
--   Complementary graded Cartier pieces over W(k)/p
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, let $k$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$, and write $W(k)$ for its ring of Witt vectors and $\mathbb{Z}_{p^2} = W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2})$ (the Witt vectors of the Galois field `GaloisField p 2`). Let $\iota \colon \mathbb{Z}_{p^2} \to W(k)$ be a ring homomorphism, let $\mathfrak{p}$ be the ideal of $W(k)$ generated by $p$, and let $\Phi$ be a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module over $W(k)/\mathfrak{p}$, that is, a commutative two-dimensional formal group law $\Phi.F$ together with an action of $\mathbb{Z}_{p^2}$ by law endomorphisms and a uniformiser endomorphism $\varpi$ satisfying $\varpi \circ \varpi = [p]$ and $\varpi \circ [a] = [a^{\sigma}] \circ \varpi$. Write $j$ for the composite of $\iota$ with the reduction $W(k) \to W(k)/\mathfrak{p}$, and assume $\Phi$ is special for $j$: its Lie algebra splits as the direct sum of the two pieces $\mathrm{lieZero}$ and $\mathrm{lieOne}$, each of which is an invertible $W(k)/\mathfrak{p}$-module. The conclusion is that the two graded pieces of the Cartier module of $\Phi.F$ in degrees $0$ and $1$ — the subgroups of those $f$ with $[\omega(c)]_*f = \omega(j(\omega(c))^{p^{n}}) \cdot f$ for every $c \in \mathbb{F}_{p^2}$, where $\omega$ denotes Teichmüller lifts and the right-hand side is the homothety action, for $n = 0$ and $n = 1$ respectively — are complementary additive subgroups of that Cartier module.
--
--   This is the splitting $M = M_0 \oplus M_1$ of the graded Cartier module of a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module at the base point of the Čerednik–Drinfel'd uniformisation, here over the base $W(k)/p$. It feeds the analysis of rigidified special formal modules and Drinfel'd data used in the comparison of the formal upper half-plane with the moduli functor.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_CerednikDrinfeld_FormalODModule_isCompl_gradedPiece_of_isSpecial_wittVector_quotient.lean

